# Task 05 — Masonry Gallery

**Category:** Layout & Structure
**Difficulty:** near-senior
**Style school:** Editorial

## The problem

A Pinterest-style image gallery with tiles of different heights that packs
together with no visual gaps, built without any JS masonry library.

## Constraints

- Pure CSS Grid dense packing only — no JavaScript layout calculation and no
  third-party masonry library.

## Key implementation decisions

- The grid uses `[grid-auto-flow:dense]`, an arbitrary property. Dense
  packing tells the grid to backfill any empty cell it can find instead of
  strictly placing items in source order — this is what produces the
  masonry look without any JS.
- `[grid-auto-rows:80px]` defines a fixed row unit. Each tile's `rowSpan`
  (1 to 3) multiplies against that unit via `[style.grid-row]="'span ' +
tile.rowSpan"`, simulating images of different natural heights.
- Tile colors come from the token scale and are stored as full class name
  strings directly in the `tiles` array (e.g. `'bg-brand-300'`) — not built
  dynamically from a variable — so the Tailwind scanner can detect them as
  literal text in the `.ts` file it already scans.

